SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 310 | Thursday, March 12, 2026

Good morning.

1) Chicago: landlords declare the bottom, tenants shrug
The theme of the industrial real-estate conference was that new construction starts have collapsed, so vacancy has to fall from here. Probably right on a two-year view. The tenant panel pointed out that the sublease pipeline is still 60 million square feet nationally and that 3PLs are consolidating into fewer, larger buildings. Rents are flat to down for the year, whatever the landlords say.

2) USTR opens a product-exclusion process for the new steel derivative tariffs
Requests due by April 30, decisions within 90 days. The last round approved about 40% of requests and took eleven months. Manufacturers importing fasteners, springs and precision stampings are the obvious filers.

3) A tire cord shortage nobody saw coming
A fire at a Southeast Asian nylon-6 plant took out a meaningful share of the tire-cord fabric supply. Tire makers say they have eight weeks of inventory. Fleets already dealing with insurance and driver costs now get to add tire allocations to the list.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 2.7. Average number of software systems a mid-size manufacturer uses to schedule a single production line, per a survey out this week. The mode was "a spreadsheet plus the ERP plus a whiteboard."

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

